{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed218530468/6/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f341894c\" lines=\"63-78\">\n<description>\nReorient each two-pixel blue/pink segment so it reads pink then blue when viewed from its border marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is dominant, with one border-only marker color and blue/pink foreground segments.\n- Every marker lies on the outer border and points inward along its row or column.\n- Along exactly one inward ray from each marker is a single adjacent two-pixel blue/pink segment.\n\nRule\n- For each marker, look inward perpendicular to the border side containing the marker.\n- Rewrite the two blue/pink pixels on that ray so the nearer one is pink and the farther one is blue.\n- Keep marker pixels, background pixels, and all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker gives a local viewing direction; the segment's required order is relative to that marker, not to global row or column order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3e6067c3\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nConnect framed squares via colored bridges following a control-pixel sequence.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.\n- Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.\n\nRule:\n- Start at the square matching first control color c0.\n- For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.\n- Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.\n\nKey insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"78e78cff\" lines=\"71-91\">\n<description>\nRecolor the row-wise passage indicated by wall pixels with the singleton marker color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one singleton marker pixel and one wall color.\n- Wall pixels lie on several rows and form left/right limits around the marker column.\n- Empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows are open horizontal passages.\n\nRule:\n- On a wall row, paint background pixels between the nearest wall strictly\n  left of the marker column and the nearest wall at or right of it; if only\n  one side is present, paint from that wall to the opposite grid edge.\n- On empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows, paint the whole row.\n- On empty rows outside the wall-row band, reuse the nearest end wall-row interval.\n- Preserve wall pixels and the original marker pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The wall color defines scanline limits relative to the marker column; the\n  marker color fills those implied horizontal spans rather than flood-filling\n  through connected background.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8886d717\" lines=\"43-63\">\n<description>\nRepair markers embedded in the main object and copy the remaining markers one pixel toward the solid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A mostly background grid contains one solid non-background edge line.\n- Apart from that edge and the background, there is one large main object\n  color and a smaller marker color.\n- Each marker is either embedded in the main object, touching it on at\n  least three 4-neighbor sides, or isolated on the background.\n\nRule:\n- Embedded markers are recolored to the main object color.\n- Isolated background markers remain in place and also paint the adjacent\n  background pixel in the direction of the solid edge line.\n- All other pixels are unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe full edge line is a direction cue: it tells which way the background\nmarkers extend, while markers surrounded by the main object are simply\ndefects to be filled.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1efba499\" lines=\"70-85\">\n<description>\nSwap paired marker pixels across the largest foreground-color barrier, placing them just outside the barrier segment.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color is the unique most frequent non-background color; its pixels form the stationary barrier.\n- Other foreground pixels are single markers, sometimes paired on opposite sides of one contiguous barrier segment in the same row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Scan each row and column that crosses exactly one contiguous barrier segment.\n- If the line has exactly one marker on each side of that segment, erase both source markers and place each marker in the cell immediately beyond the opposite side of the segment.\n- Lines with markers on only one side are unchanged; the barrier remains unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marker pairing is local to a row or column, and the destination is defined by the nearest cells outside the intervening barrier segment, not by the original distance from the barrier.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7491f3cf\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nComposite two pattern tiles into a fourth panel using flood-fill from an indicator pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- Four 5×5 panels in one row, border-separated.\n- Panel 0: cue pixels = wall boundary (8-connected, edge-to-edge, through center)\n           + one 8-isolated indicator pixel.\n- Panels 1–2: source patterns. Panel 3: blank (to fill).\n\nRule:\n- Flood-fill (4-conn) from indicator, blocked by wall → inside region.\n- Inside → non-bg from panel 1; outside → non-bg from panel 2.\n- Wall → panel 1 if non-bg; panel 2 fallback when wall is 4-connected.\n\nKey insight: The cue panel's isolated indicator and wall partition the tile into inside/outside masks that select between the two source panels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22425bda\" lines=\"58-76\">\n<description>\nList the colors of overlaid straight lines in their only possible bottom-to-top crossing order.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color. Every foreground color is the visible part\nof one full one-cell-thick horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal line.\nHigher lines may hide pixels of lower lines at crossings.\n\nRule\nRecover each full line from its visible collinear pixels. At any cell where\nmultiple recovered lines cross, the visible color is above every other line\nthrough that cell. Return the unique ordering of all line colors that respects\nthose above/below constraints and whose consecutive colors are lines that\ngeometrically cross.\n\nKey insight\nThe visible crossing colors give a partial layer order, and the intended answer\nis the unique Hamiltonian chain through the line-intersection graph from the\nbottom layer to the top layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"21897d95\" lines=\"283-301\">\n<description>\nRepaint regions from T-shaped control markers, erase the markers, and optionally rotate based on a color-9 L-marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a mosaic of monochrome regions.\n- Color 1 forms T-shaped control markers on region boundaries.\n- Some controls use three singleton color-1 pixels around a colored center; that center is a literal replacement color.\n- An optional 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker triggers 90-degree rotation of the output.\n\nRule:\n- For a color-1 T marker, the single stem points at the destination region to repaint.\n- The two diagonal cells behind the stem identify the source region; the destination gets that source region's original color.\n- For a colored-center T marker, the same stem identifies the destination, but the center color is the replacement.\n- All marker cells are filled with the final color of the source-side region.\n- A 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker controls rotation: ┐ (corner top-right) → CCW 90°, ┌ (corner top-left) → CW 90°.\n\nKey insight:\n- The stem chooses what changes, while the two diagonals behind the stem choose where the marker itself belongs.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"c4d067a0\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nComplete a grid of colored blocks to match a minimap legend in the top-left corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Top-left corner: a small sprite of single colored pixels forming a minimap pattern on a regular lattice.\n- Main area: uniformly colored square blocks (NxN) arranged on a regular grid; some positions are empty (bg).\n\nRule:\n- The legend pixels define a color pattern; each pixel's lattice position maps to a block position in the main grid via a fixed scale factor and offset.\n- Existing colored blocks confirm the scale/offset alignment.\n- For every legend pixel whose corresponding grid position is empty, place a new block of that color.\n\nKey insight: The top-left pixel pattern is a scaled-down minimap of the block layout; the mapping is inferred from blocks already present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5792cb4d\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nReverse the sequence of colors along the single 4-connected foreground path while keeping the path shape fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All non-background pixels form one multicolor 4-connected one-pixel-wide path.\n- The path has exactly two endpoints and no branches.\n\nRule:\n- Walk the path from one endpoint to the other.\n- Replace the colors on those same path cells by the original path colors in reverse order.\n- Leave every background pixel unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Nothing moves geometrically; only the color string written on the path is reversed between the endpoints.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b5bb5719\" lines=\"17-32\">\n<description>\nFill the background under a top-row red/grey span with a shrinking diagonal pattern that alternates red and grey.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has an orange background.\n- All foreground pixels are red or grey and lie on the top row.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/98c475bf.py /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/581f7754.py /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/20fb2937.py /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py; do echo \"===== $f :: main =====\"; awk '\"'BEGIN{flag=0} /\"'^def main'\"\\\\(/ {flag=1} flag{if(flag>1 && /\"'^def /) exit; print; if(/''^def main'\"\\\\(/) flag=2}' \\\"\"'$f\"; echo \"===== $f :: generate_input =====\"; awk '\"'BEGIN{flag=0} /\"'^def generate_input'\"\\\\(/ {flag=1} flag{if(flag>1 && /\"'^def /) exit; print; if(/''^def generate_input'\"\\\\(/) flag=2}' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/98c475bf.py /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/581f7754.py /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/20fb2937.py /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py; do echo \"===== $f :: main =====\"; awk '\"'BEGIN{flag=0} /\"'^def main'\"\\\\(/ {flag=1} flag{if(flag>1 && /\"'^def /) exit; print; if(/''^def main'\"\\\\(/) flag=2}' \\\"\"'$f\"; echo \"===== $f :: generate_input =====\"; awk '\"'BEGIN{flag=0} /\"'^def generate_input'\"\\\\(/ {flag=1} flag{if(flag>1 && /\"'^def /) exit; print; if(/''^def generate_input'\"\\\\(/) flag=2}' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"===== /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/98c475bf.py :: main =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"Erase the complete interior glyph and expand each endpoint marker pair into its color's full glyph.\n\n    Input structure\n    - The grid is 20x20 with background inside two same-color full-height vertical frame bars.\n    - Inside the frame there is one complete color glyph and one or more other colors appearing only as two endpoint markers in the same row.\n    - Marker endpoints are always in the first and last interior columns.\n\n    Rule\n    - Keep the vertical frame unchanged.\n    - Erase any complete interior glyph.\n    - For each endpoint marker pair, use that marker color's learned glyph template and anchor its horizontal bar on the marker row.\n\n    Key insight\n    - Color identifies which glyph shape to draw; the two endpoint pixels are not copied objects, they are anchors for reconstructing that color's full symbol.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    assert g.ndim == 2\n    height, width = g.shape\n    assert (height, width) == (20, 20)\n\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n    assert bg == 0\n    frame_color = int(g[0, 0])\n    assert frame_color != bg\n    assert np.all(g[:, 0] == frame_color)\n    assert np.all(g[:, -1] == frame_color)\n    assert np.count_nonzero(g == frame_color) == 2 * height\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    marker_jobs: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n    erased_any = False\n\n    for color0 in np.unique(g):\n        color = int(color0)\n        if color in (bg, frame_color):\n            continue\n\n        coords = np.argwhere(g == color)\n        rows = _marker_rows(coords, width)\n        if rows is not None:\n            for row in rows:\n                marker_jobs.append((color, row))\n        else:\n            assert len(coords) > 2\n            out[g == color] = bg\n            erased_any = True\n\n    assert erased_any\n    assert marker_jobs\n\n    claimed: dict[tuple[int, int], int] = {}\n    for color, anchor_row in marker_jobs:\n        for dr, c in _glyph_cells(color, width):\n            r = anchor_row + dr\n            assert 0 <= r < height\n            assert LEFT_MARKER_COL <= c <= width - 2\n            assert (r, c) not in claimed or claimed[(r, c)] == color\n            assert out[r, c] in (bg, color)\n            claimed[(r, c)] = color\n\n    for (r, c), color in claimed.items():\n        out[r, c] = color\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (att2) ---------------\n\n# Canvas and palette.\n_GEN_SIZE = 20\n_GEN_GLYPH_COLORS = [1, 2, 3, 6, 7]\n_GEN_FRAME_COLORS = (1, 2, 5, 7, 9)\n# Object counts and retry budget.\n_GEN_MARKER_COUNT_RANGE = (1, 3)\n_GEN_RETRIES = 300\n\n\n===== /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/98c475bf.py :: generate_input =====\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(_GEN_RETRIES):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate valid input\")\n===== /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/581f7754.py :: main =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Slide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg = most common color.\n    - Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n    - Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n    - Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n    - Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.\n\n    Key insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n\n    def is_border(r: int, c: int) -> bool:\n        return int(r) in (0, int(H - 1)) or int(c) in (0, int(W - 1))\n\n    # --- STEP 1) Detect anchors: isolated border pixels (non-bg) ---\n    anchors_by_color: dict[int, tuple[int, int]] = {}\n    for r in range(H):\n        for c in range(W):\n            if not is_border(r, c):\n                continue\n            v = int(g[r, c])\n            if v == int(bg):\n                continue\n            ok = True\n            for rr, cc in neighbors4(int(r), int(c)):\n                if 0 <= rr < H and 0 <= cc < W and int(g[rr, cc]) != int(bg):\n                    ok = False\n                    break\n            if ok and v not in anchors_by_color:\n                anchors_by_color[int(v)] = (int(r), int(c))\n\n    assert not (not anchors_by_color)\n\n    anchor_cells = {(int(r), int(c)) for (r, c) in anchors_by_color.values()}\n\n    # --- STEP 2) Extract all foreground connected components as candidate sprites. ---\n    comps = find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=8, monochromatic=False, return_format=\"dict\")\n\n    def component_marker_color(coords: np.ndarray) -> int | None:\n        # For multi-color sprites, the marker color is the least frequent color inside the component.\n        vals = [int(g[int(r), int(c)]) for r, c in coords]\n        cnt = Counter(vals)\n        if len(cnt) <= 1:\n            return None\n        return int(min(cnt.items(), key=lambda kv: (int(kv[1]), int(kv[0])))[0])\n\n    def marker_pixel(coords: np.ndarray, marker: int) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n        # If marker appears multiple times, prefer one attached to the main body (4-adjacent to non-marker).\n        pts = [(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in coords if int(g[int(r), int(c)]) == int(marker)]\n        if not pts:\n            return None\n        if len(pts) == 1:\n            return pts[0]\n        S = {(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in coords}\n        for r, c in pts:\n            for rr, cc in neighbors4(int(r), int(c)):\n                if (int(rr), int(cc)) in S and int(g[int(rr), int(cc)]) != int(marker):\n                    return (int(r), int(c))\n        return pts[0]\n\n    moves: list[tuple[np.ndarray, int, int]] = []\n\n    for comp in comps:\n        coords = comp[\"coords\"]\n        if coords.size == 0:\n            continue\n\n        # --- STEP 3) Decide marker color for this component. ---\n        marker_col = component_marker_color(coords)\n        if marker_col is None:\n            # Single-color component: move only if it's a singleton of an anchor color, not the anchor itself.\n            if int(coords.shape[0]) != 1:\n                continue\n            r, c = map(int, coords[0])\n            v = int(g[r, c])\n            if v == int(bg) or v not in anchors_by_color or (r, c) in anchor_cells:\n                continue\n            marker_col = int(v)\n\n        if int(marker_col) not in anchors_by_color:\n            continue\n\n        mp = marker_pixel(coords, int(marker_col))\n        if mp is None:\n            continue\n\n        ar, ac = anchors_by_color[int(marker_col)]\n\n        # --- STEP 4) Compute translation (dr,dc). ---\n        on_top_bottom = int(ar) in (0, int(H - 1))\n        on_left_right = int(ac) in (0, int(W - 1))\n        horizontal = bool(on_top_bottom and not on_left_right)  # top/bottom but not a corner\n\n        dr = 0\n        dc = 0\n        if horizontal:\n            dc = int(ac - mp[1])\n        else:\n            dr = int(ar - mp[0])\n\n        moves.append((coords, int(dr), int(dc)))\n\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    # --- STEP 5) Apply all moves. ---\n    # Clear moved components first (prevents collisions with their original locations).\n    for coords, _dr, _dc in moves:\n        out[coords[:, 0], coords[:, 1]] = int(bg)\n\n    # Paste moved components:\n    # - clip out-of-bounds pixels\n    # - do not overwrite existing non-bg pixels\n    for coords, dr, dc in moves:\n        for r, c in coords:\n            rr = int(r) + int(dr)\n            cc = int(c) + int(dc)\n            if 0 <= rr < H and 0 <= cc < W and int(out[rr, cc]) == int(bg):\n                out[rr, cc] = int(g[int(r), int(c)])\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (o5x) ---------------\n\n# CFG: palette and canvas bounds.\n_COLORS = tuple(range(10))\n_BG_COLOR_RANGE = (1, 8)\n_GRID_HEIGHT_RANGE = (10, 22)\n_GRID_WIDTH_RANGE = (8, 22)\n\n# CFG: placement isolation and anchor location.\n_NEIGHBORS4 = ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1))\n_INNER_BORDER_GAP = 1\n_ANCHOR_EDGE_MARGIN = 2\n_ISOLATION_RADIUS = 1\n\n# CFG: anchor, sprite, and retry counts.\n_ANCHOR_COUNT_RANGE = (1, 3)\n_SPRITE_COUNT_RANGE = (3, 5)\n_BODY_AREA_RANGE = (4, 13)\n_GEN_RETRY_LIMIT = 300\n\n# CFG: body geometry and shape mix.\n_BODY_HEIGHT_RANGE = (2, 5)\n_BODY_WIDTH_RANGE = (3, 7)\n_FRAME_HEIGHT_RANGE = (3, 5)\n_FRAME_WIDTH_RANGE = (3, 7)\n_FRAME_MIN_AREA = 8\n_FRAME_MAX_GAP = 3\n_FRAME_BODY_THRESHOLD = 0.28\n_WIDE_BODY_THRESHOLD = 0.55\n_SYMMETRIC_BODY_THRESHOLD = 0.82\n_SYMMETRIC_TRANSPOSE_MIN_HEIGHT = 3\n_SYMMETRIC_TRANSPOSE_PROB = 0.45\n_MARKER_INTERIOR_PROB = 0.65\n_FLIP_PROB = 0.5\n\n# CFG: optional singleton distractors.\n_DISTRACTOR_PROB = 0.15\n_DISTRACTOR_COUNT_RANGE = (1, 4)\n\n_GROW_BODY_SEEDS = [\n    {(0, 0), (0, 1), (0, 2), (1, 0)},\n    {(0, 0), (0, 1), (0, 2), (1, 2)},\n    {(0, 0), (1, 0), (1, 1), (1, 2)},\n    {(0, 2), (1, 0), (1, 1), (1, 2)},\n    {(0, 1), (1, 0), (1, 1), (1, 2)},\n]\n\n\n===== /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/581f7754.py :: generate_input =====\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(_GEN_RETRY_LIMIT):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid 581f7754 input\")\n===== /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/20fb2937.py :: main =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Replace each marker below the separator with the mapped top legend square centered on that marker.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - The background surrounds a top legend, one solid non-background separator row, and a lower marker field.\n    - The legend contains equal-size filled square sprites, with a singleton marker below each sprite and aligned to its center column.\n    - The lower field contains only background and marker colors from the legend.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Pair legend markers to legend sprites by left-to-right position.\n    - Discard the legend and separator; keep the grid width and the rows below the separator.\n    - In the lower field, replace every marker pixel by a filled square of the paired sprite color, centered at the marker location.\n\n    Key insight: The top area is a positional color legend, and the actual output is the post-separator marker field expanded by that legend.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n    sep = _solid_separator_row(g, bg)\n    mapping, size = _legend_mapping(g, sep, bg)\n\n    lower = g[sep + 1 :]\n    out = np.full_like(lower, bg)\n    radius = size // 2\n\n    for r, c in np.argwhere(lower != bg):\n        key = int(lower[r, c])\n        assert key in mapping, \"lower field marker must appear in the legend\"\n        r0 = int(r) - radius\n        c0 = int(c) - radius\n        r1 = r0 + size\n        c1 = c0 + size\n        assert 0 <= r0 and 0 <= c0 and r1 <= out.shape[0] and c1 <= out.shape[1], \"expanded square must fit in output\"\n        assert np.all(out[r0:r1, c0:c1] == bg), \"expanded squares must not overlap\"\n        out[r0:r1, c0:c1] = mapping[key]\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (o5x) ---------------\n\n# Canvas and legend geometry.\nBACKGROUND_COLOR, SEPARATOR_COLOR = 7, 6\nFIELD_SHAPE = (13, 11)\nFIELD_WIDTH = FIELD_SHAPE[1]\nSQUARE_SIZE = 3\nSQUARE_RADIUS = SQUARE_SIZE // 2\nLEGEND_HEIGHT, LEGEND_MARKER_ROW, LEGEND_COLUMN_STRIDE = 6, 4, 4\n\n# Palette and object counts.\nPALETTE_COLORS = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9]\nNUM_COLOR_PAIRS, SQUARE_COUNT_CHOICES = 3, [8, 9]\n\nEMPTY, MAX_GENERATION_ATTEMPTS = 0, 300\n\n\n===== /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/20fb2937.py :: generate_input =====\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(MAX_GENERATION_ATTEMPTS):\n        inp = _gen_attempt()\n        if inp is not None:\n            return inp\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate valid 20fb2937 input\")\n===== /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py :: main =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n    - The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n    - Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\n    Rule:\n    - For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n    - Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n    - The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n    - Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\n    Key insight:\n    - The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n    assert bg == 0\n\n    counts: list[tuple[int, int, np.ndarray]] = []\n    for color in colors_present(g, exclude={bg}):\n        rooms = _room_count_for_color(g, int(color))\n        walls = _walls_for_color(g, int(color))\n        counts.append((int(color), int(rooms), walls))\n\n    drawings = [(color, rooms, walls) for color, rooms, walls in counts if rooms > 0]\n    pad_colors = [color for color, rooms, _walls in counts if rooms == 0]\n    assert drawings\n    assert len(pad_colors) == 1\n\n    pad = int(pad_colors[0])\n    ordered: list[tuple[int, int, np.ndarray]] = []\n    for rooms in sorted({rooms for _color, rooms, _walls in drawings}):\n        group = [item for item in drawings if item[1] == rooms]\n        ordered.extend(_cover_order(g, group))\n\n    width = max(rooms for _color, rooms, _walls in ordered)\n\n    out = np.full((len(ordered), width), pad, dtype=int)\n    for r, (color, rooms, _walls) in enumerate(ordered):\n        out[r, :rooms] = int(color)\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (claude-opus-4-6) ---------------\n\n# ---- CFG: Tunable generation parameters ----\n_FRAME_COUNT_RANGE = (3, 5)           # number of colored frames per puzzle\n_ROOM_COUNT_RANGE = (1, 6)            # rooms per frame\n_TIE_PROBABILITY = 0.35              # chance of one tied room-count pair\n_GRID_SIZE_BY_FRAMES = {             # (h_min, h_max, w_min, w_max, frame_max_h, frame_max_w)\n    5: (25, 30, 25, 30, 16, 18),\n    4: (22, 30, 22, 30, 18, 22),\n    3: (20, 30, 20, 30, 22, 25),\n}\n_CELL_GAP_RANGE = (3, 13)            # min/max gap between grid lines within a frame\n_NOISE_FRACTION_RANGE = (0.04, 0.09) # fraction of total cells as noise\n_WALL_DAMAGE_FRACTION = 0.25         # max fraction of wall pixels replaced by noise\n_EXTRA_OVERLAP_RANGE = (0, 3)        # non-tied overlap pairs to force\n_OVERLAP_RETRY = 100                 # attempts to place overlapping frame\n_GEN_RETRIES = 300                   # outer retry limit\n\n\n===== /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py :: generate_input =====\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(_GEN_RETRIES):\n        result = _gen_attempt()\n        if result is not None:\n            return result\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {_GEN_RETRIES} attempts\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
